ATTEMPT: 1
---
Work inspected: commit f479de3 in a `cp -a` copy at /tmp/rc2work/rc2-second
(never mutated the shared worktree; no `git checkout/restore/stash/reset`
run there; binary never invoked; no data/ dir created).

Oracle baseline: `bash .swarm/tier3/RC2/accept.sh /tmp/rc2work/rc2-second`
ends `ORACLE PASS` (O1-O4 all green, mutation noticed by 4 shipped tests).
Confirmed independently before attacking.

REFUTED — criterion 7 (display) / "every HTMX partial that renders
one-time/big-ticket ... entries" (attack angle: dict-scoped bigticket item)

`web/templates/components/whatif/bigticket-card.html`'s RC2 diff changed
`whatif-bigticket-item`'s calling contract: it used to take the raw
`models.BigTicketItem` as dot; RC2 wraps it in `{{with .Item}}` and requires
callers to pass `(dict "Settings" $.Settings "Item" .)` so the calendar
label (`calendarMonthLabel $.Settings.StartDate .Month`) can reach
`.Settings.StartDate`. The worker updated ONE of its two template callers
(`whatif-bigticket-list` inside bigticket-card.html itself) but missed the
other, pre-existing one:

  web/templates/pages/whatif.html:341-343 (inside `{{define
  "whatif-results-with-oob"}}`, the "Big ticket items list OOB update"
  block):
    {{range .Settings.BigTicketItems}}
    {{template "whatif-bigticket-item" .}}     <- raw item, NOT the dict

`whatif-results-with-oob` is rendered by `renderWhatIfResults` /
`renderRecalcWithExtra` (internal/handlers/whatif/handlers.go:195,446),
documented in handlers.go's own comment as "Used by every user-initiated
mutation" on the what-if page (add/edit/delete income, expense, healthcare,
one-time, big-ticket, etc. — not merely the big-ticket handlers).

Reproduced dynamically. Added a throwaway test (not committed, not part of
any manifest) in the copy:

  internal/handlers/whatif/rc2_probe_test.go — builds a fixture settings
  object with one BigTicketItem, calls buildResultsPartialData +
  renderer.RenderPartial(w, "whatif-results-with-oob", data), exactly the
  path handlers.go:195/446 take.

  $ go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/ -run TestRC2ProbeBigTicketOOBRender -v
  Error rendering partial whatif-results-with-oob: template:
  bigticket-card.html:31:7: executing "whatif-bigticket-item" at <.Item>:
  can't evaluate field Item in type models.BigTicketItem
  --- FAIL: TestRC2ProbeBigTicketOOBRender (0.02s)

Impact: `internal/templates/render.go`'s RenderPartial writes directly to
the http.ResponseWriter as it executes (no buffering) and the error is
DISCARDED by renderResultsTemplate (`_ = renderer.RenderPartial(...)`), so
in production this is not a clean 500 — it is a response that already
started at 200 OK, gets truncated mid-stream at the big-ticket list (which
renders before the removed-bigticket and one-time-list OOB swaps later in
the same define block), corrupting every OOB swap that follows in the same
response for ANY mutation made while the plan holds at least one
BigTicketItem. Big-ticket items are an existing, reachable, non-exotic
feature (the add-bigticket form ships in the same file); this is not an
edge case.

Why this is in RC2's scope, not a pre-existing/backlog item: `git diff
ec42346 f479de3 -- web/templates/components/whatif/bigticket-card.html`
shows the worker's own commit introduced the `{{with .Item}}` / dict
requirement (before RC2, `.Year` was read directly off the raw item and the
raw-dot caller in whatif.html worked correctly). The regression is a direct
side effect of RC2's own display change (criterion 7: "Every DISPLAY of a
scheduled date on the one-time card, big-ticket card and lists prints the
calendar label ... instead of any year figure") and RC2's own explicit
attack angle ("does `$` inside `{{with .Item}}` reach Settings in EVERY
caller of that template?") — answer: no, one caller was missed.

Test-gap note: the shipped suite (`go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/...`)
stays green because no shipped test renders `whatif-results-with-oob` with
a non-empty `BigTicketItems`; the oracle doesn't touch templates either.
This is exactly the "enumerate every caller, don't trust the diff" class
CLAUDE.md calls out, and it was invisible to both O1-O4 and the shipped
suite.

Other criteria checked and NOT refuted (evidence, so this isn't a rubber
stamp FAIL):
- Criterion 1 (legacy decode / new-key-wins / marshal drops year keys):
  read ExpenseSource/OneTimeExpense/BigTicketItem UnmarshalJSON in
  internal/models/{income_source,one_time_expense,whatif}.go — all three
  use `raw[key] present` on a json.RawMessage map (so an explicit JSON
  `null` counts as "present", a defensible literal reading of "absent");
  legacy end_year==0 -> nil EndMonth; new keys win. Oracle's
  TestRC2OracleLegacyDecode covers mixed docs and marshal round-trip; I did
  not find a counterexample beyond the null-key corner (not tested either
  way by spec or oracle, and not a plausible real-file shape).
- Criterion 3 (shift): read shiftScheduleOffsets/resolveCurrentMonth in
  settings_current_month.go — exactly one `shiftScheduleOffsets(settings,
  elapsed)` call line; clamps income/expense at 0, retains negative
  one-time/big-ticket; fixed-date plans skip resolveCurrentMonth's shift
  entirely. Oracle's TestRC2OracleShift exercises forward, backward, same-
  month no-op, past, ended, and fixed-date; all pass against the real code
  (not just described).
- Cache-clone / double-shift: traced every caller of resolveCurrentMonth /
  cloneForCurrentMonth in settings.go (loadInternalContext, saveInternal,
  LoadContext, LoadContextWithRevision, cache double-check branches). Each
  clone computes elapsed from the manager's cached (already-shifted, fixed
  until the next real Load/Save) StartDate, so repeated clones in the same
  month are no-ops and are not cumulative — confirmed by
  TestRC2OracleManagerRoundTrip's load->save->reload->cached-load chain,
  which asserts identical offsets and an unmoved anchor on disk.
- Criterion 4/5 (engine, chain, present_value): diffed
  engine/{expense.go,loop_helpers.go,stepper.go},
  analysis/{present_value.go,budget_fit.go}, chain.go against ec42346.
  OneTimeExpensesForMonth/ApplyBigTicketItemsForMonth use `float64(month)`
  (not `/12`), identical to the old `float64(currentYear*12)` for
  year-aligned months; the stepper calls both after the chain-transition
  reassignment of `s` and before returnsFor; chain.go's rebase* functions
  take/sort by Month and drop `Month-transitionMonth < 0` exactly as years
  were dropped; present_value.go skips `<0 || >=months`. Oracle's O2d/O2e
  assert firing at month 11 and non-firing at 10/12, plus year-aligned
  equivalence (12360.00) directly against these functions.
- Criterion 6 (validation): diffed prepare/validate.go — the `Year < 0`
  check is deleted outright, not narrowed; grepped the whole tree for any
  other negative-year/month rejection (MCP overrides.go, handlers_rates.go)
  — the only Year-based validators left are Roth's (out of scope, unrelated
  struct).
- Criterion 7 templates (onetime-card.html, expense-sources-list.html):
  onetime-card.html has one caller pattern throughout (dict-based, no
  `{{with .Item}}` re-scope) and both its callers (bigticket-card.html's
  own list block is the analog; onetime is invoked identically from
  bigticket-card... correction: onetime's two invocations, in
  onetime-card.html's own list and in whatif.html:352, both correctly pass
  `(dict "Settings" $.Settings "Item" .)`) — onetime is NOT affected by
  this class of bug, only big-ticket, because only the big-ticket template
  added the `{{with .Item}}` re-scope. yearOf/div funcmap registration
  confirmed in render.go, ruling 2026-09-16a's amendment respected exactly
  as specified (int yearOf, `ge (yearOf .Item.Month) .Settings.ProjectionYears`,
  inline-edit inputs left as `div ... 12` per the ruling).
- Green (criterion 9): `go build ./... && go vet ./internal/...` and the
  oracle's shipped-suite run both green in my copy — the defect above is a
  template-execution-time error that neither `go build`/`go vet` (untyped
  html/template) nor the existing test suite exercises, which is exactly
  why it survived to this review.

Observations (not FAIL, beyond today's criteria):
- RC3 is already scoped to touch these same templates for calendar-month
